---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

// V-706 — Go SDK quickstart. Third entry in the SDK quickstart trilogy
// (TypeScript V-703 / Python V-704). Same skeleton, Go-idiomatic
// examples. Pitched at backend teams running Go services that need a
// browser leg for scrape / E2E / generate-PDF workloads.
---

<BaseLayout title="Go SDK quickstart — Driftstack" description="Drive Driftstack browser sessions from Go services: typed envelopes, context-aware cancellation, streaming events.">
  <main class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-12 prose prose-slate dark:prose-invert">
    <h1>Go SDK quickstart</h1>

    <p class="lead">
      The <code>github.com/driftstack/driftstack-go</code> module wraps
      the same REST surface as <a href="/docs/api-quickstart">the curl
      quickstart</a>. Idiomatic Go: every call takes a
      <code>context.Context</code>, returns a typed struct + error,
      and the streaming surface is a channel.
    </p>

    <h2>1. Install</h2>
    <pre><code>{`go get github.com/driftstack/driftstack-go@latest`}</code></pre>

    <p>
      Go ≥ 1.22 is supported. The module has zero non-stdlib runtime
      dependencies (it speaks raw JSON over <code>net/http</code>); no
      transitive bloat.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Construct the client</h2>
    <pre><code>{`package main

import (
    "context"
    "os"

    "github.com/driftstack/driftstack-go"
)

func main() {
    client, err := driftstack.NewClient(driftstack.Config{
        APIKey: os.Getenv("DRIFTSTACK_KEY"),
        // BaseURL defaults to https://api.driftstack.dev; override
        // for staging or self-hosted deployments.
    })
    if err != nil {
        panic(err)
    }
    _ = client
}`}</code></pre>

    <p>
      The constructor does not make any network calls. Reuse one
      client across your process — it is internally pooled via
      <code>http.Client</code> with sensible defaults (no
      reconfiguration needed for production traffic).
    </p>

    <h2>3. Start a session</h2>
    <pre><code>{`session, err := client.Sessions.Start(ctx, driftstack.StartSessionRequest{
    TargetURL:        "https://example.com",
    ProfileArchetype: "default",
})
if err != nil {
    return err
}
log.Println(session.ID, session.Status, session.LiveURL)`}</code></pre>

    <p>
      <code>session</code> is a strongly typed struct. The status
      string is constrained to the lifecycle enum
      (<code>StatusQueued</code> / <code>StatusRunning</code> /
      <code>StatusCompleted</code> / <code>StatusFailed</code>) so
      compile-time switches stay exhaustive.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Wait for the session to finish</h2>
    <pre><code>{`waitCtx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(ctx, 2*time.Minute)
defer cancel()

done, err := client.Sessions.WaitUntilTerminal(waitCtx, session.ID)
if err != nil {
    return err
}
if done.Status == driftstack.StatusCompleted {
    rec, err := client.Recordings.Get(ctx, done.ID)
    if err != nil {
        return err
    }
    log.Println("Recording:", rec.PlaybackURL)
}`}</code></pre>

    <p>
      <code>WaitUntilTerminal</code> polls every 2 seconds with
      exponential backoff up to 10s, and respects context
      cancellation — a parent timeout fires the cancellation through.
      For production loads, prefer <a href="/docs/webhooks">webhooks</a>
      over polling.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Stream live events</h2>
    <pre><code>{`events, errs := client.Sessions.Stream(ctx, session.ID)
for {
    select {
    case <-ctx.Done():
        return ctx.Err()
    case err := <-errs:
        return err
    case ev, ok := <-events:
        if !ok {
            return nil // channel closed = session terminal
        }
        if ev.Type == "navigation" {
            log.Println("navigated to", ev.URL)
        }
    }
}`}</code></pre>

    <p>
      The events channel is backed by Server-Sent Events under the
      hood. Reconnects happen automatically with exponential backoff
      on transient failures; permanent failures (auth, 4xx) surface
      on the errs channel.
    </p>

    <h2>Error handling</h2>
    <p>
      Every SDK method returns a <code>driftstack.Error</code>
      pointer on non-2xx responses. Use <code>errors.As</code> to
      branch on the structured code:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{`var dse *driftstack.Error
if errors.As(err, &dse) {
    switch dse.Code {
    case "invalid_request":
        // user-facing validation error
    case "rate_limited":
        // back off and retry
    default:
        // log + surface
    }
}`}</code></pre>

    <h2>Context propagation</h2>
    <p>
      Every method takes a <code>context.Context</code> as its first
      argument. Cancellation cascades through the underlying HTTP
      requests, so a parent deadline (or an HTTP request cancellation
      in a Gin / chi / standard <code>http.Server</code> handler)
      cleanly aborts in-flight SDK calls. No goroutine leaks even
      under heavy concurrent load.
    </p>

